\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Potential Outcomes Model (POM)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Relationship Between Counterfactual States and Causality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Empirical Strategies in Labor Economics, Angrist and Krueger (1999):
\series default
 The most challenging empirical questions in economics involve 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

what if
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 statements about counterfactual outcomes.
 Interest in these questions is motivated by immediate policy concerns,
 theoretical considerations, and problems facing individual decision makers.
 The causal relationships at the heart of many economic quesitons involve
 comparisons of counterfactual states of the world.
 Someone would like to know what outcomes would have been observed if a
 variable were manipulated or changed in some way.
 Differences in the observed outcomes for a state that occured and the unobserve
d potential outcomes for a possible state that did not occur, which is known
 as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

counterfactual,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 define the the causual effects of interest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Fundamental Problem of Causal Inference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider a binary treatment for observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The potential outcomes for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 are:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}\equiv\begin{cases}
y_{0i} & \mbox{if}\ T_{i}=0\\
y_{1i} & \mbox{if}\ T_{i}=1
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

We just assume that the treatment of 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 only affects outcomes of 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

; this is known as the stable unit treatment value assumption (SUTVA).
 We want to observe 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{0i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 The treatment effect for 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{1i}-y_{0i}
\]

\end_inset

With an i.i.d.
 sample from the population, this is a random variable with a specific distribut
ion.
 We might be interested in several features of this distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\series bold
\emph on
average treatment effect (ATE)
\series default
\emph default
 is the expected treatment effect for a randomly drawn person from the distribut
ion, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
ATE=E[y_{1i}-y_{0i}]
\]

\end_inset

The ATE is useful for treatment with broad applicability (e.g., nutrition,
 taxes, etc.), but it is not always relevant.
 This is because a large segment of the population may not be eligible for
 treatment.
 For example, the average effect of job training is not very interesting
 when the population includes millionaires.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What is the 
\series bold
\emph on
average treatment effect on the treated (ATET)
\series default
\emph default
? For those who received treatment, how did it affect their outcomes?
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
ATET=E[y_{1i}-y_{0i}|T_{i}=1]
\]

\end_inset

For what fraction of the population is the treatment effect greater than
 0? Remember, individual treatment effects are not observable.
 Only one outcome out of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{0i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is actually observed.
 We call the unobserved potential outcome the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

counterfactual.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 We can see that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=y_{1i}T_{i}+y_{0i}(1-T_{i})
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=y_{0i}+(y_{1i}-y_{0i})T_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Our hope is that averages (and other statistics) can inform us about the
 distribution of treatment effects despite these issues.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Suppose we wish to examine the effect of a college education on the present-day
 value of lifetime earnings.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $T\equiv\mbox{college education}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\equiv\mbox{PDV of lifetime earnings}$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that individuals know their potential outcomes and the cost of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is $260,000.
\end_layout

