\begin_inset Formula $T=0,1$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
3.6-1.7=1.9
\]

\end_inset

 We can also calculate the average treatment effect and the average treatment
 effect on the treated:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
ATE=\nicefrac{(.2+.8)}{2}=.5
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
ATET=.8
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Selection Bias in POM
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let us consider the following 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

switching model,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in which different groups have different expectation functions.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=\begin{cases}
E[y_{0i}]+v_{0i}=\mu_{0}+v_{0i} & \mbox{if}\ T_{i}=0\\
E[y_{1i}]+v_{1i}=\mu_{1}+v_{1i} & \mbox{if}\ T_{i}=1
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E[v_{0i}]=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E[v_{1i}]=0$
\end_inset

, i.e., some benefit more than average and others less.
 However, it is important to note that 
\begin_inset Formula $E[v_{0i}|T_{i}]$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E[v_{1i}|T_{i}]$
\end_inset

 may not equal zero.
 So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{i} & = & T_{i}(\mu_{1}+v_{i1})+(1-T_{i})(\mu_{0}+v_{0i})\\
 & = & \mu_{0}+T_{i}(\mu_{1}-\mu_{0}+v_{1i}-v_{0i})+v_{0i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where the second term is the effect of treatment on observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 We can clearly see that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
ATE & = & E[y_{1i}-y_{0i}]\\
 & = & \mu_{1}-\mu_{0}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
ATET & = & E[y_{1i}-y_{0i}|T_{i}=1]\\
 & = & \mu_{1}-\mu_{0}+E[v_{1i}-v_{0i}|T_{i}]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Think about 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{1i}-v_{0i}$
\end_inset

 as individual specific gains from treatment.
 What do we do when comparing expected outcomes of treated and controls?
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E[y_{i}|T_{i}=1]-E[y_{i}|T_{i}=0] & = & E[\mu_{0}+\mu_{1}-\mu_{0}+v_{1i}-v_{0i}+v_{0i}|T_{i}=1]-E[\mu_{0}+v_{i0}|T_{i}=0]\\
 & = & E[\mu_{1}+v_{1i}|T_{i}=1]-E[\mu_{0}+v_{0i}|T_{i}=0]\\
 & = & \mu_{1}-\mu_{0}+E[v_{1i}|T_{i}=1]-E[v_{0i}|T_{i}=0]\\
 & = & ATE+\mbox{selection bias}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The same thing arises with conditional expectations:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[y_{1i}|x]=\mu_{1}(x)
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[y_{0i}|x]=\mu_{0}(x)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Under What Conditions Can We Identify Meaningful Parameters?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Pure Random Assignment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With pure random assignment, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{0i},y_{1i}\perp T_{i}
\]

\end_inset

which implies that the distribution of potential outcomes is independent
 of treatment status.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 denote the CDF, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(y_{0i}|T_{i}=0)=F(y_{0i}|T_{i}=1)=F(y_{0i})
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(y_{1i}|T_{i}=0)=F(y_{1i}|T_{i}=1)=F(y_{1i})
\]

\end_inset

So, knowing the treatment status does not provide any information about
 potential outcomes.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In addition, there are implications for all moments of the distribution,
 i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[y_{0i}|T_{i}=0]=E[y_{0i}|T_{i}=1]=E[y_{0i}]
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[y_{1i}|T_{i}=0]=E[y_{1i}|T_{i}=1]=E[y_{1i}]
\]

\end_inset

This allows for the consideration of counterfactuals in the aggregate.
 Ideally, we would like to consider
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\widehat{ATE}=\frac{1}{N}\sum(y_{1i}-y_{0i})
\]

\end_inset

Instead, we can estimate
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{N_{T=1}}\sum y_{1i}-\frac{1}{N_{T=0}}\sum y_{0i}
\]

\end_inset

which converges in probability to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E[y_{1i}|T_{i}=1]-E[y_{0i}|T_{i}=0] & = & E[y_{1i}|T_{i}=1]-E[y_{0i}|T_{i}=1]\ \mbox{\ (by the assumption of random assignment)}\\
 & = & E[y_{1i}-y_{0i}|T_{i}=1]\ \ \mbox{(ATET)}\\
 & = & E[y_{1i}]-E[y_{0i}]\\
 & = & E[y_{1i}-y_{0i}]\ \ \mbox{(ATE)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Therefore, with pure random assignment, the difference in means identify
 the average treatment effect (ATE) and the average treatment effect on
 the treated (ATET).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Random Assignment Conditional on Observables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The other names for this condition are: ignorability of treatment, unconfoundedn
ess, and selection on observables.
 Also, how often this condition is likely to hold depends on the context.
 Now, with random assignment conditional on observables, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(y_{1i},y_{0i}\perp T_{i})|x_{i}
\]

\end_inset

So, the distribution of potential outcomes will be independent of the treatment
 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, which means that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(y_{0i}|x_{i},T_{i}=0)=F(y_{0i}|x_{i},T_{i}=1)=F(y_{0i}|x_{i})
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(y_{1i}|x_{i},T_{i}=0)=F(y_{1i}|x_{i},T_{i}=1)=F(y_{1i}|x_{i})
\]

\end_inset

By implication, the same result holds true for expectations, i.e,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E[y_{1i}|x_{i},T_{i}=1]-E[y_{0i}|x_{i},T_{i}=0] & = & E[y_{1i}|x]-E[y_{0i}|x]\\
 & = & E[y_{1i}-y_{0i}|x]\\
 & = & ATE(x)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Taking expecation over 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E[ATE(x)] & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{x}}\ \delta_{x}\cdot P(x_{i}=x)\\
 & = & ATE
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{x}=ATE(x)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If we assume overlap, then we can estimate these terms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
(The Overlap Assumption):
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\ x:0<P(T_{i}=1|x)<1
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Incomplete overlap implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{x}$
\end_inset

 is undefined.
 The given assumption suggests an obvious approach to estimation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, compare average outcomes across treatment and controls, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}_{T=1,x}-\bar{y}_{T=0,x}$
\end_inset

, to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\delta}_{x}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Weight differences by a fraction of the population for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{P(x_{i}=x)}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{N_{x_{i}=x}}{N}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is important to note that this exact matching approach is non-parametric
 since we have not imposed any structure on the functional form of the condition
al expectation function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Step 1 of the procedure above is equivalent to estimating a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fully saturated regression model,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 one that allows expectations to differ across all 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 combinations.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E[y_{i}|x_{i},T_{i}] & = & \beta_{1}x_{1i}+\gamma_{1}x_{1i}T_{i}+\beta_{2}x_{2i}+\gamma_{2}x_{2i}T_{i}+\cdots\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{x}}\beta_{x}1(x_{i}=x)+{\displaystyle \sum_{x}\gamma_{x}1(x_{i}=x)T_{i}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},\ldots,x_{K}$
\end_inset

 are indicators reflecting all possible combinations of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 However, unlike a model that is just saturated-in-
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 , step 2 has us weight differences by a fraction of the population for
 each 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 can represent a group of observations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 represents years of schooling, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
x_{0}=1(\mbox{Years}=0)\\
x_{1}=1(\mbox{Years}=1)\\
x_{2}=1(\mbox{Years}=2)\\
\vdots
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Now, suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 signifies gender and whether person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 was a high school graduate.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
x_{1}=1(\mbox{Male}=1)1(\mbox{HSgrad}=1)\\
x_{2}=1(\mbox{Male}=1)1(\mbox{HSgrad}=0)\\
x_{3}=1(\mbox{Male}=0)1(\mbox{HSgrad}=1)\\
x_{4}=1(\mbox{Male}=0)1(\mbox{HSgrad}=0)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Finally, suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is only associated with gender.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E[y_{i1}|x,T_{i}] & = & \gamma_{1}1(\mbox{Male}=1)+\gamma_{2}(\mbox{Male}=1)T_{i}+\gamma_{3}(\mbox{Male}=0)+\gamma_{4}(\mbox{Male}=0)T_{i}\\
 & = & \theta_{1}+\theta_{2}\mbox{Male}_{i}+\theta_{3}T_{i}+\theta_{4}\mbox{Male}_{i}T_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Therefore, different parameterizaitons can estimate the same fully saturated
 model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Clearly, this exact matching approach 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

controls for 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 What about an OLS procedure that only controls fully for 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

? This type of model is said to be saturated-in-
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 since it includes a parameter for every value of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}={\displaystyle \sum_{x}}\beta_{x}1(x_{i}=x)+\delta_{R}T_{i}
\]

\end_inset

This model is not fully saturated, however, because there is a single additive
 effect for 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i}$
\end_inset

 with no 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

 interactions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

While the OLS regression and exact matching estimates are inherently different,
 they are still somewhat similar because regression, too, can be seen as
 a sort of matching estimator.
 The regression estimand differs from the matching estimands only in the
 weights used to combine the covariate-specific effects, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{x}$
\end_inset

, into a single average effect.
 In particular, while matching uses the distribution of covariates among
 the treated to weight covariate-specific estimates into an estimate of
 the effect of treatment on the treated (or the average treatment effect),
 regression produces a variance-weighted average of these effects.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Important to note, the treatment-on-the-treated estimand puts the most weight
 on covariate cells containing those who are most likely to be treated.
 In contrast, OLS regression is efficient for the homoskedastic, constant
 effects linear model.
 This implies that regression puts the most weight on covarite cells where
 the conditional variance of treatment status is large.
 As a rule, treatment variance is maximized when 
\begin_inset Formula $P(T_{i}=1|x_{i}=x)=0.5$
\end_inset

, in other words, for cells where there are equal numbers of treated and
 control observations, not the 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 where most of the population lies.
 Consequently, OLS may not give us what we want if heterogeneity is present
 in treatment effects and probability of treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What if 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 includes many variables, including ones that are continuous? Then, we have
 to make assumptions about the conditional expectation function.
 The linear regression model is one choice, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[y_{i}|x_{i},T_{i}]=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\psi T_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Keep in mind that this is a very restrictive model.
 We have a linear conditional expectation if observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 does not receive treatment
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu_{0}(X_{i})=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta
\]

\end_inset

and a linear conditional expectation if observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 does receive treatment
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu_{1}(X_{i})=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\psi
\]

\end_inset

This represents a homogeneous and additive treatment effect.
 Though, instead of a linear regression model, the true functional form
 may involve:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
non-linearities in 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
interactions between 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
heterogeneous treatment effects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
selection on observables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Non-parametric estimation can be straight forward: ignore 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 without treated observation.
 It is tricky if 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is continuous or has many variables/values (even more so because we don't
 know what range of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 should be considered).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What about just obtaining an average treatment effect on the treated (ATET)?
 Well, we can relax to the complete overlap assumption to:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\ x:P(T=1|x)<1
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Takeaways
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Though weaker than RA, the ignorability assumption is itself quite strong
 in most circumstances.
 Even if the assumption holds conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, it may not hold conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\beta$
\end_inset

.
 That being said, the assumption seems to have a better chance of holding
 when we have access to a richer set of observables, but 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 can still be too rich.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Yet, everyone acknowledges the challenge of identifying meaningful parameters.
 There are two approaches we can employ, which are not mutually exclusive:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Look for data where 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is very rich
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Look for, or create, circumstances and associated data where treatment is
 plausibly random, or random conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Review
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let us recall what we need to get the average treatment effect:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Observe all variation related to treatment, 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, and potential outcomes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $P(T=1|x)$
\end_inset

 is not equal to 0 or 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We must know about the process of how observable variables influence outcomes,
 which is relevant for obtaining the correct function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We must know how treatment influences outcomes, which is also relevant for
 obtaining the correct functional form.
\end_layout

